  "segments": [
    {
      "start": 0.1,
      "end": 4.05,
      "text": "I stopped prenumbing my brow and lip clients over 2 years"
    },
    {
      "start": 4.05,
      "end": 5.62,
      "text": "ago. Here's what I've learned."
    },
    {
      "start": 5.62,
      "end": 8.42,
      "text": "For every single service, but through the years"
    },
    {
      "start": 8.5,
      "end": 17.62,
      "text": "I noticed something: anesthetic changes the texture of the skin, which makes it more difficult to see how the skin is responding to technique, as well as how it's accepting color."
    },
    {
      "start": 17.62,
      "end": 22.4,
      "text": "This ultimately led to longer appointment times and uneven healed results."
    },
    {
      "start": 22.4,
      "end": 37.02,
      "text": "When I stopped numbing every single brow and lip client, I was able to clearly see how the skin responded to my technique, which meant that I could make adjustments to stroke length, needle choice, hand speed, machine speed, or pressure in real time."
    },
    {
      "start": 37.02,
      "end": 44.65,
      "text": "This took out the guesswork of needing to try three different needles, three different hand speeds, or three different machine speeds to get the result that I wanted."
    },
    {
      "start": 44.65,
      "end": 55.35,
      "text": "And here's my biggest takeaway: if your client is experiencing more discomfort than usual, don't be so quick to blame. Oh, maybe they had caffeine, or maybe they just have sensitive skin."
    },
    {
      "start": 55.38,
      "end": 58.15,
      "text": "Instead, take a look at your technique and tools,"
    },
    {
      "start": 58.15,
      "end": 66.3,
      "text": "because great results aren't only about making sure your clients are comfortable. They're about understanding the skin you're working on and how to adjust your technique accordingly."
    }
  ]
}